#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# fpga/targets/@BOARD@/@BLOCK@.pins.xdc  --  WHERE THE DESIGN MEETS THE BOARD
#
# Scaffolded into @PROJECT@ by `fpga-flow-init` on @DATE@.
# Named by XDC_PINS in fpga/design.mk. READ IN SYNTHESIS **AND** IN
# IMPLEMENTATION.
#
# WHAT BELONGS IN THIS FILE
#   Which port lands on which package pin, at which IO standard, with which
#   electrical properties: pull-ups, drive strength, slew, differential
#   termination, and the placement of anything whose location is a board fact
#   (an IO buffer, a clock-capable input, a bank-locked primitive).
#
# WHAT DOES NOT
#   * clocks, IO delays and timing exceptions  ->  @BLOCK@.timing.xdc
#   * DRC severities and configuration properties  ->  @BLOCK@.drc.xdc
#   * anything true of the BOARD no matter what is built on it - the device,
#     the oscillator frequency, the bank voltages, the .bin style. Those are
#     board-pack keys in fpga/board/@BOARD@/board.tcl. A pin ASSIGNMENT is not
#     a board fact: it is a fact about THIS DESIGN meeting that board, which is
#     why it lives here in the target directory and not in the pack.
#
# ── WHY THE THREE-FILE SPLIT IS NOT OPTIONAL ────────────────────────────────
#
# The split is BY WHEN THE FILE IS READ, and each file's contents are the ones
# that must be true at that point:
#
#   THIS FILE   synthesis AND implementation
#   timing      implementation only  (USED_IN_SYNTHESIS false)
#   drc         implementation only
#
# This one is read in synthesis because synthesis is where the IO buffers are
# inferred and configured - the standard decides which buffer and carries the
# electrical properties - and because THE EARLIEST POSSIBLE DISCOVERY OF A
# RENAMED PORT IS WORTH MORE THAN EVERYTHING ELSE IN THIS FILE. A pin
# constraint that matches nothing costs you a synthesis, not a ninety-minute
# implementation and a bench session.
#
# The engine sets USED_IN_SYNTHESIS on each file according to WHICH VARIABLE
# NAMED IT. The file cannot set it for itself, so moving a timing constraint in
# here does not make it an implementation-only constraint - it makes it a
# constraint that is now also applied during synthesis, which is a different
# design.
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# TRAP: VIVADO DROPS A CONSTRAINT THAT MATCHES NOTHING, WITHOUT AN ERROR.
#
#   set_property PACKAGE_PIN <pin> [get_ports clk_i]
#
#   on a design whose port is called `clk` does not fail. `get_ports` returns
#   an empty list, the property is applied to nothing, the run continues, and
#   the pin is unconstrained - so the tool places it wherever it likes and you
#   find out on the bench.
#
#   RENAMING A PORT SILENTLY UNCONSTRAINS EVERY LINE THAT NAMED IT. So does
#   getting TOP wrong in design.mk, because then NONE of the ports exist and
#   this ENTIRE FILE applies to nothing - a failure whose log is identical to
#   a clean run's.
#
#   That is what `make xdc-lint` and XDC_BASELINE are for: the lint counts what
#   each constraint file actually MATCHED and compares it against a committed
#   baseline, so a constraint that stops matching is a diff rather than a
#   silence. Set XDC_BASELINE after your first run and commit the baseline
#   beside this file.
#
#   The cheap in-file version of the same guard, for the ports you cannot
#   afford to lose, is the ASSERTION BLOCK at the bottom of this file.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# TRAP: XDC IS ORDER-DEPENDENT, AND A LATER LINE SILENTLY OVERRIDES AN EARLIER
#       ONE ON THE SAME OBJECT.
#
#   Two `set_property IOSTANDARD` lines for one port is not an error and is not
#   a warning. The second wins. Constrain each port ONCE, in one place, and
#   keep this file grouped by interface so a duplicate is visible to a reader.
#
#   The order the flow reads the files in is the order design.mk lists them,
#   and XDC_EXTRA preserves its own list order. Vivado has a SECOND ordering
#   mechanism - PROCESSING_ORDER on the file object - and if anything sets it,
#   the list order in design.mk no longer describes the read order and nothing
#   reconciles the two. Do not use both.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# EVERY .xdc IN THIS DIRECTORY MUST BE NAMED BY A VARIABLE IN design.mk.
#
#   An .xdc sitting here that no variable names is an ERROR from `make check`,
#   deliberately. The flow this replaces discovered constraints by a GLOB, so a
#   file named anything else was silently invisible: present in the directory,
#   read by nothing, warned about by nobody. Combined with the trap above,
#   "never read" and "read and matched nothing" were indistinguishable.
#
#   So: to add a constraint file, add a variable that names it - XDC_EXTRA for
#   an ordered extra, XDC_OPTIONAL for a conditional one. Copying a file in and
#   expecting it to be picked up is the one thing that will not work.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Copyright (C) 2026, SoC Labs (www.soclabs.org)
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------


#### 1. THE SYSTEM CLOCK INPUT #################################################
#
# The one pin every design on this board has. It must be on a clock-capable
# input; the board pack states the oscillator frequency (oscillator_hz) and
# @BLOCK@.timing.xdc states the period - THIS file states only where it enters
# the device.

set_property PACKAGE_PIN <<FILL IN: the package pin the oscillator arrives on>> [get_ports <<FILL IN: the clock port on TOP, as the RTL spells it>>]
set_property IOSTANDARD  <<FILL IN: the standard for that bank - see io_voltage_by_bank in the board pack>> [get_ports <<FILL IN: the same clock port>>]


#### 2. EVERYTHING ELSE ########################################################
#
# Uncomment and adapt what this design has. Each shape is here because it is
# the one people get wrong, and every line is a decision this toolkit cannot
# make for you.

# ── A SINGLE-ENDED INPUT OR OUTPUT ──────────────────────────────────────────
# set_property PACKAGE_PIN <pin> [get_ports <port>]
# set_property IOSTANDARD  <std> [get_ports <port>]

# ── A BUS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# One line per BIT for the pin (the pins are not contiguous and the tool will
# not guess), and one wildcard line for the standard. The wildcard is where a
# renamed bus silently stops matching, so it is a good candidate for the
# assertion block below.
#
# set_property PACKAGE_PIN <pin> [get_ports {<port>[0]}]
# set_property PACKAGE_PIN <pin> [get_ports {<port>[1]}]
# set_property IOSTANDARD  <std> [get_ports {<port>[*]}]

# ── A DIFFERENTIAL PAIR ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Constrain the P side; the N side follows from the package. The STANDARD is
# what makes it differential, and a differential port constrained with a
# single-ended standard is an error you want at synthesis, not at the bench.
#
# set_property PACKAGE_PIN <p pin> [get_ports <port>_p]
# set_property IOSTANDARD  <diff std> [get_ports <port>_p]
# set_property DIFF_TERM   TRUE       [get_ports <port>_p]

# ── ELECTRICAL PROPERTIES ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Only where the board needs them. A pull-up on a bidirectional line that the
# PCB already pulls up is a fight between two resistors, and one of them is
# inside the device.
#
# set_property PULLUP TRUE [get_ports <port>]
# set_property DRIVE  <mA> [get_ports <port>]
# set_property SLEW   <SLOW|FAST> [get_ports <port>]


#### 3. THE ASSERTION BLOCK ####################################################
#
# WHY THIS IS HERE AND NOT IN A HOOK. Because it is about THESE ports, it
# belongs beside them, and because an XDC is read by every flow that reads this
# file - including a GUI session somebody opened by hand, where no hook runs.
#
# VERIFY IT ONCE BEFORE RELYING ON IT, AND THAT IS NOT A FORMALITY. The XDC
# parser accepts a RESTRICTED SUBSET of Tcl and rejects the rest - the same
# mechanism that rejects `create_waiver` (see @BLOCK@.drc.xdc). Whether `error`
# is inside that subset on YOUR Vivado has not been measured here, because this
# toolkit launches no tool yet. So the first time you use this block, plant a
# port name that does not exist and confirm THE RUN STOPS. A guard nobody has
# watched fail is not known to work, and a guard the parser silently declined
# to run is indistinguishable from a design with no missing ports.
#
# If your Vivado rejects it, move the assertion into fpga/hooks/pre_impl.tcl,
# where the full Tcl interpreter is available - you lose only the GUI-session
# coverage, which is the reason it is offered here first.
#
# Uncomment and list the ports this design cannot be built without. The whole
# point is that a renamed port becomes a STOPPED RUN instead of a constraint
# that quietly matched nothing.
#
# foreach p {<the ports you cannot lose>} {
#     if {[llength [get_ports -quiet $p]] == 0} {
#         error "@BLOCK@.pins.xdc: no port '$p' in this design.\
#                Either the RTL renamed it or TOP in design.mk names the wrong\
#                module - and in both cases every constraint in this file that\
#                mentions it has been silently applied to nothing."
#     }
# }

